<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan Ageng Tirtayasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1631–1695</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ageng Tirtayasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ageng Tirtayasa was ruler of the Banten Sultanate in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> from 1651 to 1683, under whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> became a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> port trading with Europeans and Asians alike. In 1677 he partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> among its princes. His son Sultan Haji turned against him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> support, and he was captured in 1683.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-ageng-tirtayasa.jpg" alt="Sultan Ageng Tirtayasa" width="270" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goverment of Jakarta Special Capital Region · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Ageng_Tirtayasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
